---
/**
 * About, “What is satvik?” The site’s framing, sourcing principles and
 * non-profit stance.
 */
import ArticleLayout from '../shared/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { websiteJsonLd, organizationJsonLd } from '../shared/seo/jsonld';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="What is satvik?"
  description="Satvik (sāttvika) living in the Indian traditions, food, practice and disposition that cultivate clarity and calm, and how this non-profit site presents it."
  path="/about/"
  eyebrow="About"
  jsonLd={[websiteJsonLd(SITE), organizationJsonLd(SITE)]}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Satvik</strong> (<em>sāttvika</em> in IAST transliteration) comes from the Sanskrit word
    <em>sattva</em>, a quality of clarity, balance and goodness that the Indian traditions describe
    as one of the three <em>guṇas</em> (qualities of nature), alongside <em>rajas</em> (restlessness,
    drive) and <em>tamas</em> (dullness, inertia). To live satvik is to cultivate sattva in what you
    eat, what you do with your body, what you allow into your mind, and how you act in the world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classical sources describe this in many places: the Bhagavad Gita devotes a whole chapter to
    how the guṇas colour food, charity, discipline and knowledge; ayurvedic texts explain how food
    and routine shape the balance of the doshas; the yoga tradition treats posture, breath and
    meditation as one ascending path. This site gathers that practical wisdom under three pillars, 
    <a href="/body/">Body</a>, <a href="/mind/">Mind</a> and <a href="/soul/">Soul</a>; with the
    first module, <a href="/body/meals/">satvik meals</a>, in active build.
  </p>

  <h2>How we present it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Source-first.</strong> Where traditional knowledge is stated, sources are cited; 
      scripture, classical texts or named modern teachers, and classical sources are kept visibly
      distinct from modern interpretation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Non-dogmatic.</strong> The traditions themselves disagree; we present them
      respectfully, with their own words, without insisting that any single school has the final say.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sanskrit, readable.</strong> Terms are given in IAST transliteration with plain
      translations, so the vocabulary stays authentic without becoming a barrier.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safety-aware.</strong> Ayurveda and yoga are powerful traditions, not substitutes for
      medical care. Content that touches health carries a
      <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> and advanced practices carry cautions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p>
    Satvik.fyi is a non-profit, volunteer-built project. It carries no advertising, sells nothing
    through this domain, tracks no one, and collects no personal data. When the shop and community
    spaces launch they will live on subdomains served by external free services, clearly labelled,
    never mixed into the reading experience. It is also not a medical, psychological or spiritual
    advisory service; please read the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Content licensing</h2>
  <p>
    Original writing on this site is made available under a Creative Commons Attribution
    (CC BY 4.0) license wherever we hold the rights, in the spirit of freely shared knowledge.
    Quoted scripture and classical texts remain the heritage of the traditions and are attributed
    to their sources and translations.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
